{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210691,"nonce":"WjpKBBQ/BiyMJ9IFBM+gza0+qbI=","title":"Tiered nbody energy drift exceeds bound under parallel test load (value miscompile)","message":"tests/e2e.rs e2e_jit_nbody (tiered, jit_threshold 5) prints `false` for `drift \u003c 0.01` in roughly 1 of 3 full-suite runs when the suite runs multi-threaded. It never fails with --test-threads=1 and passes in isolation. Reproduces at c295f05 (pre krio/beadie bump) and at 5792bac (post bump), so it is not dependency drift.\n\nUnder load the broker installs compiled tiers at different iteration counts, so the failing runs execute a different mix of interpreted and JIT'd Vec3/Body accessors and closure bodies. Some mix computes visibly different floating-point results: the bound is 0.01 on a quantity that should drift by ~1e-6, so this is not rounding noise.\n\nRepro: `for i in $(seq 8); do cargo test --release --test e2e 2\u003e\u00261 | grep -E 'FAILED|test result'; done`\n\nWhere to look: the same tier-up transition family as the OSR entry issues; suspect a value handed across an OSR entry or a devirtualised getter (CallKnownFunc inline_getter_field) returning a stale field when the install lands mid-loop.","files":null}]}